map    map_get,   guarded                 45266        46704        55489        22.63
map    map_get,   guarded+values          63659        61872        85432        30.94
tagged map,       guarded                 66883        51352        50692        25.35

Microseconds for the whole loop; ns/call from the fastest of the three.

===========================================================================================
4. Term-model facts the erasure choice turns on
===========================================================================================
--- 4.1 does a map PATTERN match a map with extra keys? ---
  #{id := _, total := _, status := _} vs a 4-key map : matched
  => a map pattern is OPEN. Ticket 27 sec7 declined row polymorphism, so a
     record type is an EXACT field set -- which the pattern alone cannot say.
  same map, with map_size(X) =:= 3 added               : no_match

--- 4.2 does a TUPLE pattern match a wider tuple? ---
  {order, _, _} vs {order, 1, 2, 99}                   : no_match
  => a tuple pattern is CLOSED. Arity is exact for free.

--- 4.3 map_get/2 in a guard, on an ABSENT key ---
  guard `map_get('__type__', X) =:= order`, key absent : guard_failed_silently
  same guard, key present and equal                    : matched
  => it fails the guard rather than raising, so ONE map_get is a complete
     discriminator for a tagged map: no is_map, no map_size, no is_map_key.

--- 4.4 is field ORDER observable in each erasure? ---
  #{b => 1, a => 2} =:= #{a => 2, b => 1}             : true
  {order,1,2}       =:= {order,2,1}                   : false
  => ticket 09 says a record type IS its field SET. A set has no order, so a
     tuple erasure must invent a canonical one and put it in the wire format.

--- 4.5 what an Elixir struct looks like coming in (ticket 06) ---
  an Elixir %Order{} term                             : #{'__struct__' =>
                                                              'Elixir.Order',
                                                          id => 1,total => 2}
  map_size                                            : 3
  => it is a map with one extra key. Under a map erasure it arrives as a
     record with an extra field; under a tuple erasure it needs conversion.

===========================================================================================
5. json:encode/1 -- ticket 16 sec4's generated encoder
===========================================================================================
Ticket 16 established json:encode/1 REFUSES tuples at any depth, and made the
encoder a codegen obligation. A record is the main thing anyone serialises.

  map    #{id=>1,total=>2,status=>open}    -> {caught,error,undef}
  tuple  {order,1,2}                       -> {caught,error,undef}
  tagged map (__type__ key)                -> {caught,error,undef}
  nested: map holding a tuple              -> {caught,error,undef}
